A||N||4||rigdonhome||Reasons to do LS1 or LS6 swap||||14||
Z||000000||rigdonhome||11-05-2001||06:48 PM||||This actually started from a vaguely related thread, so I wanted to repost it with the new direction the discussion was going.  See link below...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014916.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014916.html</A> <P>The discussion turned into why or why not to do an LS1/LS6 swap!<P>Today I stood in front of a 2000 LS1 running on an engine stand.  I've seen lots and lots of engines run, but this one was calling to me!  It's intriguing because it's so different than most engines out there.  <P>Here are the reasons I want to do an LS6.  Keep in mind that I'm setting money aside for the moment...  Through top-secret resources I can get a brand new LS6 cheaper than most people can get a high-mileage LT1.  I realize not everyone can do this.<P>1)  The LS6 is (in spite of some public -antipushrod- babble) a very high-tech engine that's putting out over 400 horse bone-stock, and will still get 25mpg (30?)in a Fiero!  Compared to the SBC's of even the '60's, this thing is the best V8 ever built in my opinion.<BR>2)  The LT1 was only produced for a few years (relative to the famous old SBC)...  What was it, '92 to '97?  The LS1 has already been around from '97 to '02 with no end in sight.  Eventually, this will be as available and affordable as the LT1.  Not today, but someday.  Whoever gets to market first with a kit will HAVE the market.<BR>3)  No one has done an LS1/LS6 yet!  The more unique it is the more attractive it is to me!<BR>4)  Did I mention it has 400 horse right out of the box ???  Drool, Drool...<P>OK, I'm sure I'll think of a whole bunch more great reasons after everyone starts flaming me!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||rigdonhome||11-05-2001||07:30 PM||||Already thought of something else...<P>The few people I know who are messing with the LS1/LS6 swaps (not just in Fieros) say the problem is that the Corvette engine is designed to bolt up to a torque tube and not a transmission...  <P>What about the Camaro/Firebird setup?<P>And what about the line of truck engines that use an iron LS block?  I'll bet my cat's left ear that there are factory-made adapters to mate the LS truck blocks to older, proven transmissions that used to mate to the old SBC.  Has anyone looked into these options?  I saw a 6.0L LS truck engine today as well as the plate that adapted it to the transmission bellhousing.  The adapter looked suspiciously like the adapters I've seen handmade and put on LS1/LS6 engines to make them fit traditional transmissions.  ???<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000002||85ZainoGT||11-05-2001||08:51 PM||||I was thinking of an ls6 as a project fiero..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  except I wanted to be the first one... so you cant do it, sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||12
Z||000003||maninblack||11-05-2001||10:57 PM||||just save up and get a Z06||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||11-05-2001||11:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rigdonhome:<BR><B>Already thought of something else...<P>The few people I know who are messing with the LS1/LS6 swaps (not just in Fieros) say the problem is that the Corvette engine is designed to bolt up to a torque tube and not a transmission...  <P>What about the Camaro/Firebird setup?<P>And what about the line of truck engines that use an iron LS block?  I'll bet my cat's left ear that there are factory-made adapters to mate the LS truck blocks to older, proven transmissions that used to mate to the old SBC.  Has anyone looked into these options?  I saw a 6.0L LS truck engine today as well as the plate that adapted it to the transmission bellhousing.  The adapter looked suspiciously like the adapters I've seen handmade and put on LS1/LS6 engines to make them fit traditional transmissions.  ???<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can bolt the engine directly to any SBC transmission. (except 1 bolt that you can get by without.) But the problems come with the fact that you need the flywheel to be .40" (I think) thicker than normal because of the differences in  position of bellhousing mounting point to the flywheel mounting point. Then because the back of the motor on a LS1 where the trans bolts up isn't extended like on a small block you either have to move the transmission forward about an inch or move the engine back and possibly have a problem with it hitting the firewall. <P>I first thought a LS1 swap would be cool when I heard a 2000 'vette put his car into 1st at about 40 coming to a stoplight (on purpose). Man did that ever sound sweet. ||||reg||1
